        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas leaf blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most yards, the Husqvarna 125BVx is the best gas leaf blower: 470 CFM and 170 MPH from a 28cc engine, cruise control and a vacuum and mulching kit, per its listing. For yards of half an acre or more, or wet leaves, the Husqvarna 150BT backpack moves 765 CFM. The EarthCraft 26cc is the budget choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best handheld gas leaf blower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Husqvarna 125BVx, for its airflow, cruise control and vacuum kit. The ECHO PB-2520 is a compact pro-brand alternative, though its Amazon listing gives few specs, and the ECHO ES-250 is the pick if you mainly need a blower vacuum for beds and corners.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a backpack or handheld gas blower better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Handhelds suit driveways, patios and lawns up to about half an acre. Backpacks carry larger engines on a harness, so they move far more air and are much less tiring for large yards and wet leaves. Poulan Pro aims its PR48BT backpack at yards of half an acre and up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What CFM leaf blower do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For driveways, patios and small yards, 400 to 500 CFM is plenty, the range of the handhelds here. For larger lawns and wet leaves, look for a backpack with 600 CFM or more, like the Husqvarna 150BT at 765 CFM. Air speed in MPH matters less than CFM for moving volume.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gas leaf blowers better than battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas blowers are better for long sessions, large yards and wet leaves, because they run as long as you have fuel. Battery blowers are quieter, start instantly and need no fuel mixing, which suits small yards and noise-restricted areas. Some towns restrict gas blowers, so check local rules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What fuel does a gas leaf blower use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most handheld and backpack blowers have 2-cycle engines that run on gasoline mixed with 2-cycle oil, usually at 50:1. The PROYAMA listing specifies a 50:1 pre-mix. Use fresh fuel, mix in small batches and add stabilizer if it will sit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a leaf blower vacuum replace a leaf vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For beds, corners and small lawns, yes. Handheld blower vacuums like the ECHO ES-250 and Husqvarna 125BVx shred leaves into a bag. For large lawns, vacuuming with a handheld is slow, and a dedicated leaf vacuum or a mower with a bagger is faster.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
